<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Green Gang</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Qing Bang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Green Gang was a Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">secret society</a> that grew out of the boatmen's brotherhoods of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a> and became the dominant criminal organisation of early 20th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>. Under bosses such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huang-jinrong">Huang Jinrong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/du-yuesheng">Du Yuesheng</a> it ran opium, gambling and labour rackets from the French Concession. It allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a>'s Nationalists, supplying the men for the 1927 massacre of communists in Shanghai.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The society had its roots in the Luojiao, a Buddhist sect founded by Luo Qing in the mid-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>. In the early 18th century three sworn brothers, Weng Yan, Qian Jian and Pan Qing, brought the sect to the workers who carried grain along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a>, where Luoist groups mixed with the boatmen's societies and provided burials and hostels. In 1768 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a> ordered their temples destroyed and proscribed the sect, which went underground on the grain fleets themselves.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion</a> and the change in the course of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> around 1855 ended grain shipment on the canal. The boatmen scattered into the Taiping and Nian rebellions or into salt smuggling on the coast, and in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangsu">Jiangsu</a> in the 1870s boatmen and smugglers formed the Anqing Daoyou, the direct precursor of the Green Gang.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> suited the gang. It had become a transshipment point for grain and, as a treaty port, a gateway for foreign trade, opium included; the separate jurisdictions of the International Settlement and the French Concession favoured organised crime, and immigrants depended on hometown and sworn-brother networks through which the gang worked. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/du-yuesheng">Du Yuesheng</a>, introduced to the older leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huang-jinrong">Huang Jinrong</a> because his mentor came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a> like Huang, it controlled crime across the city.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> often hired the gang to break up union meetings and strikes. Its leader Ying Guixin was involved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a>'s assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-jiaoren">Song Jiaoren</a> in 1913, and under the name of the Society for Common Progress the gang, with other criminal gangs, carried out the massacre of about 5,000 pro-Communist strikers in April 1927 ordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a>, who later made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/du-yuesheng">Du Yuesheng</a> a general. The gang shared its drug profits with the Kuomintang through the Opium Suppression Bureau.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the Nationalist defeat on the mainland in 1949 the gang left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> and in the early 1950s opened heroin refineries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>, but it lost the struggle with local syndicates and had disappeared by the mid-1950s. Chaozhou syndicates from Swatow took over the market with the help of its chemists, and by the end of the 1960s Hong Kong chemists had set up the first laboratory for high-grade no. 4 heroin on the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Green Gang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The French police tolerated and employed <b>Green Gang</b> bosses such as Huang Jinrong and Du Yuesheng, and political exiles found refuge there: the Chinese Communist Party held its first congress in the concession in 1921.</blockquote><a href="/state/shanghai-french-concession">Shanghai French Concession</a></li></ul></section>
